{"book":{"id":"book-exposing-slavery-photography-human-bondage-and-the-birth-of-modern-visual-politics-in-america-ma","slug":"exposing-slavery-photography-human-bondage-and-the-birth-of-modern-visual-politics-in-america-ma","title":"Exposing Slavery: Photography, Human Bondage, and the Birth of Modern Visual Politics in America","authors":[{"id":"person-matthew-fox-amato","name":"Matthew Fox-Amato"}],"isbn13":["9780190663933"],"subjects":["Society & Culture"],"topics":["American Civil War","Slavery & Emancipation","Black History & Culture","Soldiers, Veterans & Combat Experience"],"centralFigures":[],"links":{"amazon":"https://www.amazon.com/s?k=Exposing%20Slavery%3A%20Photography%2C%20Human%20Bondage%2C%20and%20the%20Birth%20of%20Modern%20Visual%20Politics%20in%20America%20Matthew%20Fox-Amato","bookshop":"https://bookshop.org/search?keywords=Exposing%20Slavery%3A%20Photography%2C%20Human%20Bondage%2C%20and%20the%20Birth%20of%20Modern%20Visual%20Politics%20in%20America%20Matthew%20Fox-Amato","indiebound":"https://www.indiebound.org/search/book?keys=Exposing%20Slavery%3A%20Photography%2C%20Human%20Bondage%2C%20and%20the%20Birth%20of%20Modern%20Visual%20Politics%20in%20America%20Matthew%20Fox-Amato","worldcat":"https://search.worldcat.org/search?q=Exposing%20Slavery%3A%20Photography%2C%20Human%20Bondage%2C%20and%20the%20Birth%20of%20Modern%20Visual%20Politics%20in%20America%20Matthew%20Fox-Amato","publisher":"https://openlibrary.org/works/OL21184504W"},"sourceIds":["source-award-record-lincoln-prize-civil-war-era-gettysburg-college-previous-winners-of-the-gilder-lehrman-lincoln-pr","source-open-library-exposing-slavery-photography-human-bondage-and-the-birth-of-modern-visual-politics-in-america-ma","source-open-library-summary-exposing-slavery-photography-human-bondage-and-the-birth-of-modern-visual-politics-in-america-ma"],"publicationYear":2019,"readerProfile":{"readerLevel":"crossover","narrativeScore":0,"accessibilityScore":0,"scholarlyScore":0,"traits":[],"method":"deterministic_catalog_heuristics_v1"},"renownProfile":{"knowsBook":true,"publicFame":18,"criticalRenown":66,"controversy":12,"llmAffinity":76,"affinityResidual":4.32,"confidence":{"publicFame":0.88,"criticalRenown":0.78,"controversy":0.72,"llmAffinity":0.88},"tags":{"craft":"craft-of-explanation","evidence":"archival-research","stance":"revisionism"},"model":"gpt-5.6-luna","promptVersion":1},"summary":"\"Within a few years of the invention of the first commercially successful photography process in 1839, American slaveholders had already begun commissioning photographic portraits of their slaves. Ex-slaves-turned-abolitionists such as Frederick Douglass also came to see how sitting for a portrait could help them project humanity and dignity amidst northern racism. In the first decade of the medium, enslaved people had begun entering southern daguerreotype saloons of their own volition, posing for cameras, and leaving with visual treasures they could keep in their pockets. And, as the Civil War raged, Union soldiers would orchestrate pictures with fugitive slaves that envisioned racial hierarchy as slavery fell. In these ways and others, photography powerfully influenced how bondage and freedom were documented, imagined, and contested. This book explores how photography altered, and was in turn shaped by, conflicts over bondage. Drawing upon an original source base that includes hundreds of unpublished and little-studied photographs of slaves, ex-slaves, and abolitionists as well as written archival materials, it puts visual culture at the center of understanding the experience of late slavery.","subjectCategories":[{"source":"open_library","scheme":"open_library_subject","label":"Slaves, united states, social conditions","sourceId":"source-open-library-summary-exposing-slavery-photography-human-bondage-and-the-birth-of-modern-visual-politics-in-america-ma"},{"source":"open_library","scheme":"open_library_subject","label":"Portrait photography","sourceId":"source-